WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.568
-You prepared
for State of the Unions

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.904
when you were working with
the Bush presidency.

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:07.173
-Yeah.
-How do you think

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:11.711
they're preparing for Tuesday,
and how do you feel about --

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:13.479
It seems like people
are looking back

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:15.949
at the Bush State of the Unions
in a different way now.

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:18.885
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:21.354
-Look, we -- we --

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:24.290
We didn't usually have
high expectations --

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.158
You know, he was -- Bush --
I loved Bush.

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:27.560
[ Laughter ]
He wasn't known --

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.930
He wasn't known for these, like,
soaring rhetorical skills.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.665
-Right.
-But his speeches

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:35.601
were thoughtful,
and he would consult

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:38.037
with his own party,
and he'd try to have policies,

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:39.639
and there were bipartisan things

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:42.141
that we knew both parties
would support.

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:44.210
This White House
is a little more

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.812
fly by the seat
of their pants,

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:48.915
and they have a guy named
Stephen Miller,

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:50.049
who you've talked about.
-Yeah.

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.417
-You cover Stephen Miller.
-Yeah.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.086
-Who isn't really known
for doing anything

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:55.488
that unites anybody.

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.157
The unique thing
about this speech

00:00:57.157 --> 00:00:59.425
is that they are on their heels.

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:00.793
They are on their knees.

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:02.728
They're in such a position
of weakness.

00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:04.564
He's going to have
to do something

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:07.466
to sort of shake up
his own political standing.

00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:08.701
-Isn't it terrifying?

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.269
And I know this happens
all the time.

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:12.238
Is there nothing more --
or I would say --

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.073
I feel like the most terrifying
thing in the world

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:16.976
is, like, Donald Trump needing
to do something to shake it up.

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:19.645
-Yeah. Please.
-Please, yeah.

00:01:19.645 --> 00:01:20.946
-Like, just somebody
going in like,

00:01:20.946 --> 00:01:22.348
"We've got to change the game."

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:24.751
You're like, "No, I don't --"
-No, please don't.

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.519
-Yeah.

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.788
Just, like, maybe like go back
and read the rules of the game.

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:29.955
-Right. [ Laughs ]

00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:31.791
-Just to, like, see, like,
what's normal.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:32.959
-Right.
[ Applause ]

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:34.560
-Have Pence do it.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.230
-Pence do it.
I can't im--

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.332
I feel like he's going to do
the whole State of the Union,

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:41.267
like, looking back at Pelosi.
-Right, right.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.669
-The fact that she's
over his shoulder.

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:43.970
-Yeah.
-I mean, she can --

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.838
-That relationship
is so interesting.

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:48.441
I mean, she --
she became Speaker

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.476
while my old boss
was still president.

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:53.146
And so I've been to this movie,
I know how it ends --

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.682
well for her, not well for
the Republican president.

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:59.719
And the fact that no one
studied sort of her --

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:01.454
not just her
legislative acumen,

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:03.255
but she can do what
really no one else

00:02:03.255 --> 00:02:04.490
in either party can do --

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.924
she can hold her party
together.

00:02:05.924 --> 00:02:08.260
And I don't know if it's all
of her experience.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.563
I don't know if they
fear/respect her.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:13.132
It's usually a combination of
that in politics, at least.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:15.067
But she can hold
her party together

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:17.770
the way no other
Democrat or Republican can.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.339
-Well, I think in that way
that there are --

00:02:20.339 --> 00:02:22.742
I think there are Republicans
who thank God right now

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.143
that there is
Mitch McConnell,

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:25.812
because Mitch McConnell
gets things done.

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:27.046
I mean, if you're a Republican
right now --

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:28.247
-Or keeps them
from getting done.

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:30.149
-Yes.
-Can we boo? Can we boo?

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:31.718
-You can do whatever you
want, you're my guest.

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.385
-All right, he said
Mitch McConnell! Boo!

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:34.787
-All right...
[ Booing ]

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.656
-Okay, all right, sorry.
-But I mean, like,

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:39.292
it seems like Nancy Pelosi
is his -- you know, that, like,

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.261
you ultimately need somebody
who understands how it works.

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:44.230
And it seems to me that
Nancy Pelosi and Mitch McConnell

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.831
understand how things work.
-Definitely.

00:02:45.831 --> 00:02:47.767
-Whereas when you have
somebody like --

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.769
Donald Trump does not
fundamentally understand

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:51.637
how it works, and anything
he's getting accomplished

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:53.406
is through the help of somebody
like Mitch McConnell.

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:54.507
-Yeah, everything.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.776
And everything
that he fails to do,

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:59.578
it comes from --
is born from that incompetence.

00:02:59.578 --> 00:03:01.781
And if it isn't covered
on "Fox &amp; Friends,"

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:03.683
he hasn't learned it yet,
two years in.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.284
-Yeah.
-I mean, that is his whole

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:06.719
sort of going to school.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.222
And to their credit,
this week, they finally said,

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:11.624
"Whoa, you are not --" I think
they said, "You are not a king."

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.126
I mean, he's even
getting pushback

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.061
from his trusted sources
of information.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:18.064
-There's a lot of talk about
how the media is going to cover

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:21.500
the 2020 election, and how --

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.102
you know, the danger
of talking about it

00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:24.971
like horse race politics
and whatnot.

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:27.506
But isn't the person
that's most likely

00:03:27.506 --> 00:03:30.276
to be distracted by
the 2020 election

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:31.944
the president himself?
-Of course.

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:35.014
And it won't even be the
substantive attacks on his --

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.682
sort of the cruelty
and vapidness

00:03:36.682 --> 00:03:38.351
of his policy and his tweets.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:40.086
It will be the person
with the biggest crowds.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.054
So Kamala should be the person

00:03:42.054 --> 00:03:43.556
that Democrats are most
excited about.

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.558
She has the thing
that scares him the most,

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.126
the biggest crowd.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.661
-And he's probably just
sitting there trying to come up

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.162
with a nickname for her
right now.

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:52.164
-I mean, you --
definitely.

00:03:52.164 --> 00:03:55.068
And if you go back to the first
day of his presidency

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.136
where he sent his
press secretary out to lie,

00:03:57.136 --> 00:04:00.305
he went to the CIA
and sort of debased the CIA

00:04:00.305 --> 00:04:01.874
by bragging about
his crowd size.

00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:05.577
She now had the thing that we
all know he values the most,

00:04:05.577 --> 00:04:06.912
the biggest crowd.
-Yeah.

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:08.514
Well, it's certainly going to be
interesting to watch.

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:09.983
We'll be happy to watch you
talk about it.

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:12.285
Thank you so much for being
here. It's always a pleasure.

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:13.519
Thank you as well, Rosie.
-My pleasure.

